## For human

This section is for me to keep track of what I need to manually review, feel free to add to this

- [ ] The fresh-install then airplane-mode walk on a REAL handset. Owed since 0.3.0 and still owed: the suite does the emulated equivalent under Playwright (install the bundles, go offline, open a page this profile has never seen), which is not the same thing and is not claimed to be
- [ ] Look at a formula image on a phone and in a document. The suite proves the PNG decodes at the right size and has ink on it; only a person can say whether the typography reads well at the size they actually paste it
- [ ] Sanity-check the calorie figures against one external TDEE calculator — the arithmetic is unit-tested against the published equations, but nobody has compared the whole answer to another tool
- [ ] Run a real photo through the colour extractor at 4, 8 and 16 colours and say whether the palettes look right. Synthetic flat-band images prove the maths; only a photograph proves the judgement
- [ ] Look at a mosaic and a swirl next to the reference images in the Placeholders TODO lines and say whether they land
- [ ] Decide about the `@emnapi/core` override in package.json. It has no recorded reason, and it is what makes npm crash while resolving vitest's peer set, which is currently blocking a patch update
  - [ ] This was a requirement for CI to function on github pages, if you have a better solution to not cause it to crash, I'm all ears

## Per change
Required guidence to follow per change

- Check off changes as you go
  - Make sure the TODO is kept up to date so that if a usage limit is hit I'm not left in the dark
- if data model is updated
    - There are adequete tests for the system to ensure no data migration issues, and that the system works both from the UI and has unit tests
- Add Changes to the CHANGELOG.md
    - If a version exists and does not have a release date yet, keep adding to it, if there's no versions without a release date, then bump version (e.g. `v0.1.0 -> v0.2.0`) in the version file, and add a new main heading to CHANGELOG.md for it (e.g. `v0.2.0 (unreleased)`) and add the changes under there.
    - Be brief, 2-4 setnences per change, and if you need more, prompt for approval from the user
    - Add all changes under the following categories as sub-headings:
      - Features
      - Bug Fixes
      - Other
        - For things like
          - Documentation improvements
          - Refinements to existing systems that weren't bugs
          - anything else that doesn't fit in the other sections